1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,872
In 1986, motorsport's governing body did something it had never done before: it banned an entire category of car, mid-season, after two seasons of escalating tragedy. This is the story of Group B rallying, and how it ended.

2
00:00:15,872 --> 00:00:45,070
Group B, introduced 1982: minimal homologation limits, near-unlimited power ambition.

3
00:00:45,070 --> 00:01:00,377
By the mid-1980s, factory Group B cars were putting out anywhere from roughly three hundred thirty to over six hundred horsepower, depending on the car and its final evolution, packed into bodies that weighed close to a thousand kilograms.

4
00:01:00,377 --> 00:01:22,311
Narrow roads, minimal barriers, spectators close to the line.

5
00:01:22,311 --> 00:01:39,769
5 March 1986, Rally Portugal, Sintra: 3 spectators killed, 30+ injured.

6
00:01:39,769 --> 00:01:54,960
Works teams withdrew from the remainder of the event; crowd-safety questions intensified.

7
00:01:54,960 --> 00:02:01,430
Less than two months after Sintra, tragedy struck again -- this time inside the competition itself.

8
00:02:01,430 --> 00:02:23,013
2 May 1986, Tour de Corse, stage 18: Toivonen and Cresto killed after their car left the road and caught fire.

9
00:02:23,013 --> 00:02:31,123
This rally is insane, even though everything is going well at the moment. If there is trouble, for sure, I am completely finished.

10
00:02:31,123 --> 00:02:46,888
FISA announces Group B banned for 1987, replaced by Group A.

11
00:02:46,888 --> 00:03:04,029
Investigation: driver reaction time and vision were outpaced by the cars' speed.

12
00:03:04,029 --> 00:03:26,062
The cars outran the drivers, the roads, and the crowd-safety margins built to contain them.

13
00:03:26,062 --> 00:03:51,135
Group A: lower power ceilings, higher homologation numbers, formalized spectator control.

14
00:03:51,135 --> 00:04:06,138
A category outpacing its safety margins gets a rule change, not a warning -- a philosophy that still holds today.

15
00:04:06,138 --> 00:04:14,188
That's the story of the weekend Group B died. If you want more motorsport history like this, subscribe to Apex Archive.
